Author: frank <hellmann@pik-potsdam.de>
Date:   Mon Nov 23 01:53:16 2026 +0100

    Compiler: a choice left to the run among methods at a set is not consistent; `hasmethod`'s negative at a set is not folded
    
    A membership adds no method (compiler §5.5): a call whose matches include a method at a set has the same matches
    after the membership, and code that leaves the choice to the run stays valid, so the walk keeps it. Two answers
    fold that choice, or a signature's coverage, at compile time with nothing that records it. A call whose method is
    chosen when it runs among matches one of which rests on memberships (a signature that names a set, or has a
    variable with a lower bound) answers by the world's memberships, yet its effects were consistent: a caller
    evaluated it at compile time at a constant (a singleton member) and kept the answer after the membership (E-542's
    pattern 7, `disp` and `app` compiled once at a set or an abstract type). And `hasmethod` folded "no method covers
    these types" with a missing-method edge, which the walk keeps where the method matched before though it did not
    cover. Now such a call, and such an `applicable`, are not consistent (`choice_rests_on_memberships`: row 187's
    rule for an answer about a set that inference does not fold), and `hasmethod`'s negative folds only with the
    absence edges of the pairs the lookup dropped at a final pair; where a method that meets the types rests on
    memberships, or the types name a set, it is not folded.
    
    Reference: compiler §4.8, §5.5.
    Test: experiments/implementation/w171/gates/n44_set_choice_not_folded.jl.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

